AB  - Autonomous Security Operations Centers (SOCs) increasingly use Large Language Models (LLMs) and Reinforcement Learning (RL) to automate cyber threat detection and incident response. However, training both Red Team and Blue Team agents at the same time often leads to unstable learning. The attacker and defender continuously change their strategies, which results in inconsistent behavior, slow progress, and policy failure. This study suggests an alternating adversarial training framework for LLM-based network incident response. In this approach, one agent is frozen while the other is optimized. The defender trains against a fixed attacker until it reaches stability. After that, the improved defender is frozen, allowing the attacker to adapt and find new strategies. This alternating process is repeated to ensure stable learning while gradually increasing the challenge of the training environment. The framework is implemented in a ThreatGraph-based enterprise network simulation that reflects realistic incident response processes, such as triage, investigation, remediation, and reporting. The experimental evaluation compares the proposed alternating training strategy with traditional simultaneous adversarial training in the same environments, reward systems, and starting conditions. Performance is measured based on training stability, convergence speed, defender strength against new attack scenarios, and the rate of policy failure. This approach aims to show that alternating adversarial optimization offers a more stable and effective training method for building strong and adaptable autonomous cyber-defense agents.
ER  - 

