AB  - The growing sophistication of Advanced Persistent Threats (APTs) and the industrialization of AI-driven offensive tools have rendered traditional Security Operations Centers (SOCs) structurally insufficient. Reactive, rule-based defenses cannot match the speed and adaptability of autonomous attack systems. This paper presents TriShield, a vision-driven framework supported by a proof-of-concept implementation for autonomous and self-evolving cyber defense in which three complementary AI-driven components operate within a unified closed adversarial feedback loop. Component A (Predictive SOC) employs a Transformer-based model for real-time network traffic classification, achieving F1=0.991 on CICIDS-2017. Component B (CAIP) implements a causally-constrained DQN agent that simulates APT lateral movement with 100% success rate on a 4-node enterprise network. Component C (Adversarial LLM-CTI) fine-tunes SecureBERT+ on NVD/CVE descriptions, achieving 73.44% macro F1-score on zero-day CVEs (2024–2025) with 75.31% under 20-shot conditions. The loop is validated through a proof-of-concept scenario: after one autonomous feedback cycle, the SOC false negative rate drops from 2.4% to 0.84% and attack detection improves from 1/4 to 4/4 with minimal human intervention. TriShield addresses the isolation problem of existing cybersecurity modules by orchestrating them into a self-improving adversarial loop. This work presents a proof-of-concept validation based on a single autonomous feedback cycle; multi-cycle stability analysis constitutes future work. (Abstract)
