AB  - Penetration testing remains a critical component of modern cybersecurity, enabling the proactive identification of system vulnerabilities before exploitation by adversaries. However, traditional penetration testing is highly dependent on expert knowledge, manual coordination of tools, and time-intensive analysis, limiting its scalability and consistency. This study proposes a hybrid multi-agent penetration testing framework based on Large Language Models (LLMs) to enhance automation and decision-making in offensive security workflows. The research includes a systematic analysis of recent advances in LLM-driven cybersecurity, multi-agent architectures, reinforcement learning integration, and vulnerability assessment techniques. A conceptual architecture is developed, incorporating specialized agents, structured memory, tool-oriented reasoning, and a validation layer with human-in-the-loop supervision. The proposed system is experimentally evaluated in a controlled environment using standard penetration testing tools and an LLM interface. The results demonstrate improved efficiency in reconnaissance analysis, vulnerability interpretation, and report generation, with observed reductions in analysis time. However, limitations related to contextual consistency, reliability, and adversarial robustness persist. The study contributes a structured and safety-aware framework for LLM-assisted penetration testing and outlines key challenges and future directions for autonomous cybersecurity systems.
