AB  - The rapid proliferation of autonomous Large Language Model (LLM) agents has introduced a largely uncharacterized attack surface in production AI deployments. Unlike static model inference, agentic pipelines expose dynamic execution paths, persistent memory, and third-party tool integrations that adversaries can exploit through carefully crafted inputs. This paper presents a structured threat model for autonomous LLM agents operating in cybersecurity-sensitive environments. The research objective is to identify, formalize, and mitigate attack vectors unique to agentic LLM pipelines that existing security frameworks fail to address. We formalize five primary attack vectors: prompt injection chaining, tool misuse via intent manipulation, goal drift through context poisoning, memory persistence exploitation, and cross-agent trust escalation. We provide empirical demonstrations using three publicly available agentic frameworks—AutoGPT, LangChain, and CrewAI—and propose CyberShield-A, a three-layer containment architecture comprising input sanitization, execution sandboxing, and behavioral auditing. Evaluation across 47 red-team scenarios demonstrates that CyberShield-A reduces successful attack completion rates by 73.4% while preserving 91.2% task utility. A full 2^3 ablation study confirms that all three layers contribute independently significant protection (p<0.001 for each). These findings establish a foundational taxonomy and deployable architecture for securing the next generation of autonomous AI systems.
