AB  - The increasing deployment of autonomous Large Language Model agents in enterprise platforms, cloud services, and intelligent automation systems has introduced new cybersecurity challenges associated with prompt injection, unauthorized reasoning manipulation, and chained-agent compromise. Existing digital forensic methodologies are ineffective in reconstructing distributed AI-agent execution behavior because modern LLM ecosystems generate fragmented contextual traces, temporary reasoning states, and decentralized interaction logs. Current forensic systems primarily analyze static operating-system artifacts and network events, making them unsuitable for AI-native infrastructures. To address these limitations, this paper proposes Forensic LLM-Trace, an interpretable forensic architecture designed for hostile-operation reconstruction within multi-agent LLM ecosystems. The proposed approach integrates semantic artifact normalization, confidence-oriented attribution evaluation, graph-driven relationship reconstruction, and automated threat-intelligence reporting into a unified analytical pipeline. Neo4j-based interaction mapping is employed to visualize malicious prompt propagation between interconnected agents, APIs, and memory contexts. Additionally, transformer-assisted entity extraction improves contextual forensic interpretation by identifying hidden hostile instruction transitions embedded within communication chains. Experimental evaluation using simulated prompt-manipulation scenarios demonstrates improved timeline reconstruction precision, reduced false attribution, and enhanced forensic transparency compared with conventional log-oriented examination systems. The proposed architecture establishes a scalable investigative foundation for future AI-native digital forensic environments.
