AB  - Adversarial pressure on digital infrastructure is accelerating faster than the supply of skilled penetration testers, exposing a structural gap that manual testing alone cannot close. This survey examines the emerging body of work on AI-driven penetration testing, covering reinforcement learning (RL) agents, large language model (LLM)-based systems, machine learning-assisted vulnerability discovery, and hybrid architectures that combine multiple paradigms. We analyze seven representative systems and approaches—including PentestGPT, HackingBuddyGPT, AutoAttacker, CyberBattleSim, and IAPTS—characterizing each by demonstrated capability, evaluation environment, and measurable limitation. Our three primary contributions are: (1) a structured taxonomy of AI-driven penetration testing approaches organized around agent architecture and reasoning strategy; (2) a comparative analysis table that maps each system to standardized dimensions, enabling direct cross-system comparison; and (3) an identification of four cross-cutting open problems—hallucination in exploit generation, evaluation benchmark fragmentation, the simulation-to-reality transfer gap, and the regulatory vacuum around autonomous offensive tooling. We close by proposing directions for future work including agentic long-horizon planning, formal exploit verification, and federated security learning.
