AB  - The present day cybersecurity environment is marked by an ever-increasing asymmetry between offense and defense. As software systems become increasingly complex, so too is the “window of exposure” for critical vulnerabilities. This is particularly true to the point that human penetration testing is no longer sufficient to keep pace with system complexity. Conventional approaches to Vulnerability Assessment and Penetration Testing (VAPT) are limited by the scalability and determinism of conventional automated scanners. This research proposes the design and development of an Autonomous Offensive Cyber Security Framework that incorporates Large Language Models (LLMs) to automate all aspects of penetration testing. We present here a novel concept for a Recursive Cognitive Loop (RCL) that incorporates an LLM to serve as an orchestrator that replicates the “Observe Orient Decide Act” (OODA) loop of human thought. By leveraging a Shadow Graph for state management and Retrieval-Augmented Generation for real-time exploit retrieval, this framework is shown to be capable of autonomously narrowing the scope of attacks from broad segments of the network to specific parameter-level exploits.
ER  - 

