AB  - The rapid transition from passive large language models to autonomous agent-based coding systems, including frameworks like Claude Code and OpenClaw, has significantly accelerated software development cycles while simultaneously complicating the enterprise attack surface. Conventional security paradigms relying on perimeter defenses and static role-based access control (RBAC) are inherently designed for deterministic entities, leaving them critically underequipped to mitigate threats posed by non-deterministic algorithmic agents. These autonomous systems frequently generate unpredictable, multi-step sequences of system calls upon ingesting poisoned external environment data or hidden prompt injections. This article proposes a novel Zero Trust architectural framework, complete with rigorous mathematical formalization, specifically engineered for secure multi-agent ecosystems. The architectural core features an intelligent Semantic Gateway deeply integrated with operating system-level coordination modules to dynamically provision ephemeral sandboxing environments. To achieve continuous authorization for asynchronous transactions, the framework utilizes a mathematical structure based on Heterogeneous Graph Neural Networks (HGNN). This algorithm continuously computes an agent's dynamic trust tensor by quantifying the topological divergence between its predicted and actual traversal paths within the deterministic space of a corporate repository graph. To empirically validate the analytical efficacy of this advanced architecture, the research team executed a large-scale simulation processing 100,000 asynchronous transactions. The proposed framework demonstrated exceptional predictive power, achieving a true positive rate (TPR) exceeding 98.7 % in the detection and preventive isolation of critical remote code execution vulnerabilities, including sophisticated CVE-2025-61260 exploits. Crucially, this robust security posture was maintained with a minimal false positive rate (FPR) of 1.1 % and an average processing overhead of just 45 milliseconds. Ultimately, this paradigm modernizes the NIST SP 800-207A standard by replacing static binary access controls with a continuous Markov decision process, transforming stochastic AI unpredictability into a quantifiable engineering asset for critical infrastructures.
ER  - 

