AB  - Autonomous cybersecurity is evolving past the one-shot, static LLM-based attacker. What comes next is even scarier: adversarial frameworks that connect large language models to external tools, persistence and memory, and recursive task splitting/decomposition, creating fully fledged planning-and-learning systems capable of performing actions in a cyber environment, observing the results, and making new plans, almost without human intervention. Autonomous agents are not bringing any new exploit techniques; what they are doing is automating the process. Their value lies in the economic disruption – they decrease the marginal cost of an attack to zero, move attacker expertise from a scarce resource to an oversight role, and give cybercriminals the asymmetry because "good enough" autonomous output works most of the time. In this paper, we conduct a narrative review of the 2022–2026 literature on autonomous attack agents, LLM-powered red-teaming, and agent-driven attack pipelines. Our contribution includes: (i) functional decomposition of autonomous agents into cognition and executive support services, (ii) a lifecycle map including reconnaissance, social engineering, and post-compromise activities, (iii) a definition and discussion of operational uplift U = T_manual/T_AI and its implications for defender economics, and (iv) countermeasures based on the concept of the analyst bottleneck – rate limiting, automation of workflow verification, and identification of agent-generated output. The main message of our paper is that the true frontier of cyber risks lies not in a model, but in its agent system ecosystem – wrappers, plugins, and frameworks – where the transition from capability demonstration to adversarial utility is the shortest.
ER  - 

