AB  - Penetration testing simulates hacker attacks to find vulnerabilities in information systems. Recent Large Language Model (LLM)-based pentesting systems show encouraging results on benchmarks, but their behavior in real deployment is hard to trust due to three intertwined problems: (1) their plans often contradict themselves within a single session, (2) agents tend to retry slight variants of failed commands instead of trying something new, and (3) the same prompt on the same target can lead to very different outcomes from one run to the next. To address these problems, we present MAPT, a memory-augmented multi-agent framework for automated penetration testing. MAPT runs agents in a plan-verify-act-reflect loop, built around three core components. The Strategic Planner compiles high-level objectives into a type-checked attack tree, where every leaf is a sub-goal that can be verified by a regex or a command exit code, so that each action is grounded in a structured plan rather than improvised mid-session. A Dual-Memory Module holds past trajectories on one side and a knowledge base of exploitation patterns on the other; this lets the agent recall what has already failed and pull in the right primitive for the current situation, instead of cycling through minor variants of the same failed command. A dedicated Critic agent reviews each command before it runs and labels each outcome afterwards, using a small fixed label set so that its decisions vary much less from run to run. In addition, a lightweight Tactical Executor deterministically translates each leaf sub-goal into a shell command, isolating strategic reasoning from low-level synthesis. Extensive experiments on AutoPenBench and NYU CTF Bench show that MAPT outperforms state-of-the-art baselines in both task success rate and cross-run stability.
ER  - 

