AB  - The growing complexity, magnitude, and occurrence of cyberattacks have revealed critical shortcomings in the conventional approaches to cybersecurity training which are typically based on fixed datasets and pre-determined attacks. Such strategies are not sufficient to equip security systems and practitioners with zero-day, adaptive, and emerging threats. To overcome this difficulty, this paper suggests an artificial intelligence (GenAI)-based model of synthetic cyber threat simulation and defence training, which would allow forming realistic and diverse, as well as dynamically changing environments of attacks without exposing sensitive organizational data. The proposed framework uses the developed large language models based on transformers, generative adversarial networks, and diffusion models to generate high-fidelity threat artifacts, including phishing schemes, patterns of malware behaviour, network intrusion evidence, and social engineering tactics. The system can optimise attack simulation through the integration of the reinforcement learning-inspired adversarial agents to simulate behaviour of the real-world threat actors in order to enhance the resilience and versatility of defensive mechanisms. Moreover, the architecture is designed with privacy-protective features like federated learning and differential privacy to enable sharing of threat intelligence in collaboration, without having to disclose the data. We perform automated feedback on defence performance on the basis of major cybersecurity indicators, such as detection rates, response time, resilience, and false-positive rates, in addition to adaptive training of security analysts and AI-based defence systems. Through experimentation, it is established that the suggested GenAI-based simulation environment will significantly increase the preparedness to threats, decrease training expenses, and better detectives than traditional rule-based cyber range models. The framework is scalable and can be used to deploy across cloud-edge environments, thus it can be applied to enterprise security operations centres, critical infrastructure protection, and autonomous cyber protection ecosystems. This publication underscores the radical nature of generative AI to redefine cybersecurity as a reactive protection to proactive, intelligence-driven resilience in cybersecurity, thus providing the basis of the next-generation cyber defence training platforms.
ER  - 

