AB  - This paper defines a methodology for using pre-trained large language models (LLMs) to solve capture the flag (CTF) challenges. This investigation is motivated by recent developments introducing frameworks that allow LLMs to execute a set of tools to solve complex problems. The goal is to design and implement realistic red agents capable of executing state-of-the-art offensive tactics/techniques to train blue agents and human cybersecurity experts in gyms, emulated environments, and cyber ranges. Our solution uses a LLM (Claude 3.5 Sonnet) as a CTF Agent, integrating reasoning and tool usage in an iterative loop. In addition, our solution uses a fine-tuned LLM (Quantized LoRA) to automate the generation of code for vulnerability scanning scripts (Mistral 7b). This model interacts with the CTF agent, leveraging cyber threat intelligence to scan the attack surface and identify key areas for penetration testing, thus bridging the gap between CTF-based challenges and offensive security deployments. Our solution is evaluated on an open benchmark with 200 challenges categorized into cryptography, forensics, binary exploitation (pwn), reverse engineering, web exploitation, and miscellaneous, as well as a quantitative evaluation of the fine-tuned Mistral 7b. Experimental results suggest that LLM-based red agents can successfully automate offensive tactics/techniques using the same set of tools available to pen-testers.
ER  -
