AB  - This paper introduces the Autonomous Attack Chain (AAC), a framework designed to conceptualise and model the distinct phases of offensive cyber behaviour executed by artificial intelligence (AI) agents. Optimised for human-led attacks, traditional cyber kill chain frameworks often inadequately capture the dynamic, adaptive, and cognitive capabilities inherent in modern AI systems. The AAC addresses this gap by synthesising foundational theories, including related cognitive architecture models such as Belief-Desire-Intention, Observe–Orient–Decide–Act, Goals-Plans-Action, and the Perceptual Control Theory. Conceptualised as a continuous, cyclical process centred around an AI agent’s Cognitive Core (a generative AI model), the AAC outlines phases such as Planning Initiation, Data/Tool Acquisition, Execution and Adaptation, Persistence/Propagation, and Impact Optimisation. This model explicitly accounts for the agent’s ability to reason over uncertain environments, form internal representations of success, and dynamically adjust actions based on environmental feedback, embodying the adaptive and learning affordances crucial to autonomous cyber operations. Thus, the AAC provides a better understanding of AI-driven threats, laying essential groundwork for developing advanced, adaptive defensive strategies.
ER  - 

