AB  - The rising rate of cybersecurity threats has made traditional, manual penetration testing increasingly cumbersome, resource-demanding, and difficult to perform across complex network structures. This paper presents an AI-driven automated penetration testing framework, Pentest MCP, that combines Large Language Model (LLM) inference with the Model Context Protocol (MCP) to enable intelligent coordination of cyber security tools. The framework lets users trigger security evaluations through natural language instructions, which an LLM-based agent interprets to generate structured penetration-testing workflows. The proposed architecture comprises three layers: an AI reasoning layer, an MCP communication layer, and an execution-and-analysis layer. The reasoning layer performs intent analysis and dynamically selects appropriate security tools; the MCP layer standardizes JSON-RPC communication between the AI agent and external utilities; and the execution layer integrates tools such as Nmap, Nikto, WhatWeb, and DNS-enumeration utilities to automate reconnaissance, service discovery, web-technology fingerprinting, and vulnerability testing. Empirical evaluation in a controlled penetration-testing environment shows the framework effectively detects vulnerabilities such as outdated software, misconfigured services, and denial-of-service vectors, and generates organized reports with severity ratings and remediation guidance. The findings indicate that AI-assisted orchestration substantially improves the efficiency, scalability, and automation of vulnerability-assessment workflows.
