AB  - The integration of Large Language Models (LLMs) into cybersecurity has enabled autonomous agents capable of dynamic vulnerability exploration. However, existing agent frameworks (e.g., ReAct, AutoGPT) operate with implicit reasoning, producing decisions that are largely opaque and difficult to audit. We introduce CAIP-Pentest, a framework that structures autonomous penetration testing through an explicit causal decision loop: Observation → Hypothesis → Action → Result (O-H-A-R). By design, every action is justified by a verifiable security hypothesis, enabling full traceability and native interpretability. We formalize the agent’s reasoning as a dynamic Causal Attack Graph and validate the framework via a lightweight Proof of Concept. Experimental results from a proof-of-concept evaluation conducted on OWASP Juice Shop and DVWA across six vulnerability scenarios suggest that CAIP-Pentest maintains competitive exploration coverage while achieving significantly higher interpretability scores than baseline approaches while providing structurally auditable decision traces, addressing a critical gap between LLM autonomy and cybersecurity accountability.
ER  - 

