AB  - Recent advancements in Large Language Models (LLMs) have driven significant progress in cybersecurity applications, particularly in automating Capture the Flag (CTF) challenge-solving. We present LD-CIPHER, a multi-agent LLM framework for collaborative CTF challenge solving. LD-CIPHER introduces Planner-Executor agent system, consisting of a Planner agent that divides a large task into several small tasks and multiple heterogeneous Executor agents that receives information from the small tasks and solves them. Additionally, LD-CIPHER incorporates the scoring system, which improves problem-solving by generating several sub-plans and selecting the best sub-plan to generate a higher-quality prompt. We evaluate LD-CIPHER on CTF benchmarks using multiple LLM models and conduct comprehensive studies to highlight the impact of our enhancements. Our results demonstrate that the multi-agent LD-CIPHER system achieves a significant improvement in challenges solved, setting a state-of-the-art performance on one benchmark: 18.0% and 0.09$ cost on NYU CTF Bench.
ER  - 

