AB  - Phishing attacks remain one of the most pervasive cybersecurity threats, with email-based phishing responsible for over 90% of global data breaches. Traditional text-based detection systems achieve moderate success in identifying linguistic cues but fail to capture visual deception strategies such as brand impersonation, domain typosquatting, and counterfeit login layouts. This paper introduces an autonomous multi-agent framework that leverages reasoning capable Vision-Language Models (VLMs) for end-to-end phishing detection and remediation within Gmail environments. The proposed agent continuously monitors spam folders, analyzes rendered email screenshots, and autonomously executes remediation actions permanently deleting confirmed phishing messages (confidence ≥ 0.80) or restoring legitimate ones to the inbox. Experiments evaluate reasoning VLMs (Qwen2-VL-2B, Qwen2.5-VL-7B) against standard architectures (LLaVA-1.6, Moondream2, Phi-3.5-Vision) across 150 real-world phishing samples. Results show that reasoning-enhanced VLMs achieve 82–85% detection accuracy, reducing false positives by approximately 15% compared to standard VLMs (58–63% accuracy). Furthermore, integrating a lightweight reasoning LLM (Qwen-3-4B-Thinking) improves multimodal consistency, yielding an additional 3% accuracy gain and greater semantic alignment between visual and textual cues. This study advances the field of agentic AI for cybersecurity by demonstrating the practicality of fully autonomous, multimodal phishing email management on commodity hardware. The complete implementation publicly available at: https://github.com/rafi79/AI-Phishing-Detector.
ER  - 

