AB  - The integration of LLMs via the Model Context Protocol (MCP) introduces privacy risks where intermediate tool outputs may expose sensitive assets. In sensitive scenarios such as penetration testing, critical information about attack chains and target assets can be leaked to third-party model services. Existing privacy methods mainly focus on filtering user inputs and fail to protect dynamic tool-generated context. To address this, we propose MCP Privacy Middleware (MCPPM), a lightweight privacy framework for the Local MCP + Remote LLM architecture. MCPPM is deployed at the local trust boundary as a bidirectional proxy to sanitize context streams. It combines hybrid NER (regex + domain-adapted Transformer) to identify security entities, an entity fusion mechanism to reduce semantic fragmentation, and a context-aware safe-zone strategy to selectively mask sensitive information while preserving reasoning semantics. Experiments on a cybersecurity dataset show that MCPPM achieves an F1 score of 0.903 and a semantic utility score of 0.921 with 101 ms average latency. Our results demonstrate that MCPPM enables secure deployment of MCP-based autonomous agents in zerotrust environments.
ER  - 

