AB  - Autonomous transportation systems face a critical challenge in handling "long tail events," which are rare and unpredictable scenarios that are underrepresented in training data and difficult to anticipate during testing. Unlike human operators, who can flexibly adapt to novel situations, current autonomous systems lack the contextual awareness to respond effectively. To address this limitation, this paper presents the use of Large Language Model (LLM) and Vision-Language Model (VLM)-based anomaly detection and notification system integrated into an existing autonomous electric golf-cart platform developed at James Madison University. Building on prior work in autonomous vehicle development, the proposed system continuously monitors internal system state, user interactions, and visual data from exterior and interior cameras to detect dangerous or unusual situations and relay concise alerts to a remote human operator.This work further investigates the trade-offs between on-vehicle and cloud-based inference pipelines, evaluating key metrics such as latency, robustness, cybersecurity exposure, and privacy risk. Adversarial threats, including telemetry spoofing and video input manipulation, are examined, and corresponding defenses are proposed. The project culminates in the development of a secure, functional prototype alongside a planned public dataset release designed to benchmark safety detection performance and false-alarm rates, fostering reproducible research across the autonomous systems community.
ER  - 

