AB  - Penetration testing is a complex and resource-intensive process that requires deep technical expertise and extensive tooling. Recent advances in Generative Artificial Intelligence (Gen-AI) and Large Language Models (LLMs), particularly when combined with the Model Context Protocols (MCP), promise to automate penetration testing workflows and lower entry barriers. However, the rapid evolution of this field has created challenges in orientation and assessment. This paper addresses the gap by presenting a systematic methodology for discovering and selecting open-source Gen-AI-based penetration testing tools and classifying them. We conducted a comprehensive GitHub repository search using 14 queries and collected 939 results. By defining and applying rigorous selection criteria, we identified 13 relevant and actively maintained tools. These tools were systematically categorized using a novel categorization framework, covering nine categories: report generation, model support, Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) capabilities, state management, interaction models, Penetration Testing Execution Standard (PTES) phase coverage, extensibility, communication interfaces, and deployment models. Our analysis shows that most tools remain in proof-of-concept stages, with MCP wrappers around established penetration testing suites being the most prevalent option. This work provides essential insights for both newcomers and experienced security professionals navigating this emerging field.
ER  - 

