AB  - Cybersecurity risk assessment frameworks for critical infrastructure have traditionally focused on network-centric vulnerabilities, with limited integration of Artificial Intelligence (AI)-specific risks. As hybrid AI models are increasingly embedded in Autonomous Cyber Defense (ACD) systems, a standardized approach to evaluate both network and AI components is needed. This paper proposes a framework for automating cybersecurity risk assessment that jointly considers conventional infrastructure assets and AI models. It extends established frameworks by introducing structured asset taxonomies, fuzzy risk metrics, and AI-specific threat modeling dimensions. The approach is designed to support Software-Defined Defense (SDD), which implements interfaces to monitor and control multi-layer military systems across strategic, deployed, and tactical domains. The framework integrates Cyber Threat Intelligence (CTI), vulnerability databases (CVE/CWE), and adversarial Tactics, Techniques, and Procedures (TTPs) into an automated workflow, enabling risk reasoning over both AI and traditional network assets. Empirical validation using two use cases, backdoor attack and LLM poisoning, demonstrates the framework’s ability to assess evolving risks in mission-critical infrastructures using AI systems.
ER  - 

