AB  - Security operations centers increasingly rely on threat hunting to complement reactive alert handling and to turn investigative findings into detection rules ready for deployment. However, large language models used as analyst assistants may produce incomplete, outdated, or incorrect statements, and the resulting rules can be syntactically valid yet operationally unusable. This paper presents THAITI, a locally deployed multiagent prototype for threat hunting using AI agents and threat intelligence. THAITI combines persistent investigation memory with a verifiable rule synthesis loop for Wazuh and Suricata. The system records intermediate artifacts using versioned schemas, validates them automatically, and links every material claim in hypotheses and reports to extracted evidence such as events, documents, and knowledge graph elements. Instead of generating rules directly, THAITI produces constrained intermediate representations, compiles them deterministically into Wazuh and Suricata rules, and validates the rules on replayed telemetry and, when required, in an isolated laboratory environment. We describe the THAITI architecture, evidence retrieval strategies, and an evaluation methodology that emphasizes rule executability, false positive rate, and end to end traceability from conclusions to underlying evidence.
ER  -
