AB  - The escalating sophistication of cyber threats necessitates a paradigm shift from static, signature-based defenses to proactive, intelligent, and adaptive security measures. Honeypots, a foundational tool in cyber deception, have traditionally been limited by their static nature, rendering them susceptible to fingerprinting and evasion by skilled adversaries. This paper presents a novel framework for a cognitive, adaptive honeypot system that leverages an agentic Generative AI framework to create a dynamic and evolving deception environment. Our system architecture integrates distributed medium-interaction Cowrie honeypots with a periodic data pipeline using the MQTT protocol, which transfers attacker activity logs to a central analysis module for deep semantic analysis. This module, whose logic is partitioned into several functional roles, interprets attacker intent, identifies complex Tactics, Techniques, and Procedures (TTPs), and autonomously generates tailored bash scripts to dynamically reconfigure the honeypot’s environment, thereby enhancing its realism and prolonging attacker engagement. A Pinecone vector database provides the system with long-term contextual memory, enabling the recognition of recurring attack patterns and facilitating more sophisticated, stateful deception strategies. Comparative analysis demonstrates that our adaptive honeypot detains attackers over 400% longer than a static counterpart, captures a significantly richer dataset of malicious activities, and successfully elicits more advanced attack tools and payloads. This research pioneers a closed-loop, AI-driven deception-feedback mechanism, laying the groundwork for next-generation autonomous cyber defense systems.
ER  - 

