AB  - Security Operations Centres (SOCs) process tens of thousands of alerts each day, yet industry surveys consistently show that sixty to eighty per cent are non-actionable false positives. This chronic noise burden drives analyst fatigue, delays incident containment, and suppresses detection of genuine threats. Rule-based SIEM correlation and static signature systems fail to generalise to evasive techniques such as Living-Off-the-Land Binaries and Scripts (LOLBAS) or previously unseen multi-stage intrusion chains. This paper presents the LLM-Augmented SOC Analyst, a human-centred, privacy-preserving framework that integrates structured SIEM alert ingestion with a curated multi-source threat knowledge base (MITRE ATT&CK, LOLBAS, Sigma rules, analyst annotations), dense semantic retrieval via Qdrant and SentenceTransformers, and locally hosted LLaMA 3 inference for binary alert classification and natural-language justification. Evaluation on a 1000 alert benchmark corpus yields 87 per cent accuracy, precision 0.85, recall 0.89, and an F1 score of 0.87, outperforming a rule-only SIEM baseline by 16 percentage points and a machine-learning classifier without retrieval augmentation by 8 points. False-positive rates declined by 15 per cent and triage throughput improved by roughly one third. All computation remains on-premise, satisfying GDPR, HIPAA, NIS2, and ISO 27001 data-residency requirements. We analyse failure modes, adversarial RAG risks, ethical considerations, and outline a roadmap toward agentic SOAR integration.
ER  - 

