AB  - Network penetration Testing (or pentesting) is a positive way to find vulnerabilities associated with the increasingly complex world of IoT, Cloud, and 5G. In this discussion paper, we examine the evolution of declining penetration testing tools, from simple tools such as Nmap and Metasploit, to the emergence of sophisticated Artificial Intelligence (AI)-powered platforms like Darktrace and GuardiCore. We discuss the categories of known vulnerabilities, including misconfiguration, weak authentication, and outdated software, and how different AI model architectures, particularly reinforcement learning and large language models, can be leveraged to address components of the mission, such as automating reconnaissance, identifying vulnerable systems, and generating exploitation vectors. Although AI will increase speed, scale, and ease of use, challenges such as trust, explainability, and robustness against adversarial attacks remain significant hurdles. We underscore the necessity of a hybrid, human-in-the-loop model where AI augments human expertise, a framework often mandated by compliance and ethical considerations. In this respect, we identify the need for a strong explainability framework and a consideration of ethical issues and auditability aspects throughout any automated testing framework. Lastly, we suggest future planning for research and development into kinds of autonomous (self- learning) frameworks that will grow with changing adversarial threats using AI, while also lamenting the diminishing role of humans to augment (collaborate with) AI, in what is an important field of cybersecurity.
ER  - 

