AB  - The increasing deployment of sensor-driven automation in industrial equipment has led in the collecting of huge volumes of operational data for monitoring and maintenance purposes. However, obtaining usable insights from this data remains difficult due to its complexity and unstructured nature. This study examines how recent cutting-edge research tackles the use of Large Language Models (LLMs) in the construction of AI-powered agents capable of autonomously reading telemetry data and maintenance records to facilitate predictive maintenance in industrial systems. The proposed architecture to be built along these series of 5 articles, shall combine LLMs with structured query techniques and domain-specific knowledge embeddings, allowing the AI agent to detect abnormalities, forecast failures, and produce technician-friendly reports. The AI agent improves decision-making processes by utilizing natural language understanding (NLU), pattern recognition, and real-time data synthesis, allowing for preventative maintenance and better equipment performance. We also investigate technical issues like data pretreatment, knowledge representation, and the alignment of LLM reasoning with domain-specific restrictions. This study emphasizes the transformative potential of AI-driven automation in industrial technologies and predictive maintenance by closing the gap between raw sensor data and actionable insights. It is the first of a five-part series that lays the framework for future research into real-time monitoring, adaptive learning models, and industrial AI deployment tactics.
ER  - 

