AB  - The complexity of cyber threats is rapidly outpacing the capacity of human analysts, creating an urgent need for intelligent augmentation in cybersecurity. As cybercriminals employ increasingly sophisticated tactics, traditional methods of threat detection and response are becoming inadequate. This situation necessitates a paradigm shift in how cybersecurity is approached, particularly through the integration of Artificial Intelligence (AI). Large Language Models (LLMs) can be positioned not as autonomous systems but as cognitive assistants that enhance human analysts’ capabilities throughout the cyber kill chain. By translating vast, unstructured data into actionable intelligence, LLMs can provide contextual understanding, enabling analysts to make faster, more informed decisions and transition from a reactive to a proactive defensive strategy. The primary challenge lies in the overwhelming volume of data and the speed at which cyber threats evolve. The objective of this project was to explore the role of LLMs as cognitive assistants in cybersecurity, focusing on their ability to enhance the efficiency and effectiveness of human analysts. By leveraging LLMs, we aimed to improve threat identification, incident response, and overall cybersecurity posture, ultimately shifting the focus from merely reacting to threats to anticipating and mitigating them proactively. To achieve these objectives, we employed a comprehensive methodology that involved the collection of diverse datasets, including open-source intelligence, threat reports, and historical incident data. The LLM was connected to perform cybersecurity tasks using an MCP server, enabling it to generate insights and recommendations tailored to the needs of human analysts. The results of our project indicate that the integration of LLMs significantly enhances the capabilities of cybersecurity analysts. By providing real-time analysis and contextual understanding, LLMs enable faster triage of alerts and more accurate threat assessments. Our recommendations include the continued development and integration of LLMs into cybersecurity workflows, emphasizing the importance of human oversight and collaboration.
