AB  - Digital forensic investigations are increasingly challenged by the growing volume, velocity, and complexity of digital evidence, which can delay investigations and overwhelm forensic resources. While existing approaches have advanced the reconstruction of technical events, they provide limited support for systematically reasoning about criminal intent, a critical factor in legal attribution and prosecution. This study introduces the Intention Recognition for Digital Forensics (IRDF) framework, a legally grounded and implementation-independent model that integrates technical evidence interpretation with intent-based legal reasoning. The framework formally represents crimes, intent categories, evidentiary elements, actions, and states, providing a structured foundation for intention-aware forensic analysis. To evaluate its feasibility under controlled experimental conditions, the framework is realized through an agentic AI implementation supported by a structured Criminal Knowledge Base (CKB). The implementation was evaluated through a scenario-based experimental study spanning phishing, denial-of-service, and ransomware investigations, multiple intent categories, repeated executions, and three Large Language Models (OpenAI, Gemini, and DeepSeek), under both knowledge-guided and baseline conditions. The results indicate that structured knowledge guidance improved intent-classification accuracy across all evaluated models, increasing accuracy from 33.3% to 66.7%, and 44.4% to 75.0% and 47.2% to 69.4% for DeepSeek, Gemini, and OpenAI, respectively. At the same time, unsupported intent inflation was substantially reduced from 66.7% to 22.2%, and 55.6% to 11.1%, and 44.4% to 19.4%, respectively, resulting in more disciplined, explainable, and legally aligned reasoning behavior across models. The findings demonstrate the potential of knowledge-guided agentic AI to function as an intelligent forensic assistant capable of supporting increasingly complex investigations while maintaining necessary human oversight. The study contributes both a reusable formal framework for intention-aware digital forensic reasoning and an empirically evaluated agentic AI realization that demonstrates its potential to practical feasibility.
ER  - 

