AB  - Large Language Models (LLMs) have shown considerable potential for supporting cybersecurity incident response. However, their deployment in operational cybersecurity helpdesk remains constrained by unreliable workflow execution, hallucinated mitigation recommendations, and security risks arising from adversarial inputs and sensitive information exposure. This paper proposes a trustworthy Agentic Retrieval-Augmented Generation (Agentic RAG) architecture for AI-assisted cybersecurity incident pre-triage that integrates multi-agent orchestration, grounded retrieval with citation validation, and layered guardrails within a unified workflow. The architecture supports incident classification, standards-grounded mitigation recommendation, ticket management, and user notification using authoritative cybersecurity knowledge derived from NIST SP 800-61, MITRE ATT&CK Enterprise, and BSSN Regulation No. 1 of 2024. The proposed architecture was evaluated in terms of workflow reliability, retrieval quality, and security robustness using Task Completion Rate (TCR), the RAGAS framework, and adversarial jailbreak and personally identifiable information (PII) protection experiments. Experimental results achieved a 94.3% TCR across 35 incident-report scenarios, together with average RAGAS scores of 0.924 Faithfulness, 0.858 Answer Relevancy, and 0.983 Context Relevance. The rule-based guardrail independently blocked 78.6% of adversarial jailbreak prompts before LLM invocation, while the complete defense-in-depth pipeline achieved a combined blocking rate of 99.79%. These results provide evidence that the integrated architecture can support AI-assisted cybersecurity incident pre-triage with reliable workflow execution, grounded recommendations, and layered security controls.
ER  - 

