AB  - Security monitoring of industrial control system (ICS) network traffic requires the classification of cyberattacks into standardized taxonomies such as MITRE ATT&CK ICS. Supervised machine learning approaches achieve high accuracy but require labeled training data for every cyberattack technique they are expected to recognize. The MITRE ATT&CK ICS taxonomy defines 83 techniques, but no public ICS dataset provides labeled examples for more than a few of them, making comprehensive supervised training infeasible. Supervised models further require retraining when deployed on a new ICS network. To overcome these limitations, this paper introduces a tool-augmented large language model (LLM) approach for post-hoc classification of cyberattacks in ICS networks without labeled training data (zero-shot). The LLM autonomously investigates network alerts using a set of tools for time series analysis, PCAP inspection, baseline comparison, and MITRE technique lookup. A two-step classification separates attack method identification from MITRE technique mapping. The approach is evaluated on 450 true-positive alerts from a physical ICS testbed across nine attack methods and six MITRE ATT&CK ICS techniques with a frontier cloud model (GPT-5.4) and three local open-weight models from the Nemotron-3 series. The best results are achieved by the frontier model with an effective accuracy of 89% over all alerts.
