AB  - Into the domain of AI-assisted system administration, LLM-based agents are increasingly used to automate operational routines and security tasks on Linux systems. Different studies have demonstrated the capabilities of using LLMs to execute shell commands, analyze logs, support penetration-testing workflows, fix code and vulnerabilities, indicating a growing interest in LLM-driven automation for cybersecurity-related operations. The aim of this paper is to preliminary evaluate the capabilities and effectiveness of LLMs in managing a Linux node using a dedicated agent from the point of view of security topics. Related to this context, we focus on the use of local LLMs, as relying on public LLM APIs may expose sensitive data — such as personal information or company infrastructure details — retrieved from server environments to external AI providers. The primary target and application of our work are therefore environments where data confidentiality, compliance, or sensitive infrastructure necessitate on-premise solutions. Our findings indicate that even local LLMs with relatively limited capabilities, in terms of the number of parameters, can provide a reliable and effective solution for managing Linux servers from security perspective and supporting system administrators in their operational tasks.
