AB  - The current cloud-native and distributed computing systems produce enormous amounts of system logs that are heterogeneous at network, host, and application layers. Despite providing highly useful evidence to distinguish cyber threats, such logs are unstructured, highly variable, and large-scale, making them challenging to analyze manually and limiting the capabilities of traditional Security Information and Event Management (SIEM) systems. Traditional rule-based application detection and monolithic machine learning applications have problems with scalability, contextual knowledge and high rates of false positives in dynamic environment. To address these shortcomings, this paper introduces an LLM-Orchestrated multi-agent system of log-based detection and analysis of cyber threats that includes the generation of structured signals, contextualization of features, and intelligent agent-driven reasoning. The suggested framework initially uses an asynchronous log ingestion mechanism which enables the processing of large volume log streams on a scaled basis. Raw logs are then converted to structured signals by Drain3 based online log template mining and regex-based normalization. The signals are augmented with contextual threat data and past behavioral patterns, and then statistical anomaly detection is performed using z -score analysis. These signals are then mapped to corresponding MITRE ATT&CK techniques and fed to a central orchestration layer which then sends them to specific analytical agents that combine deterministic detection rules with reasoning using large language models to validate threats. The results of experimental assessment performed on 6000 raw logs constituting known and unknown attack cases show that the framework results in detection rates of 98.5 and 92.7 respectively and enhances scalability and reliability in log-based cyber threat detection.
