AB  - Honeypots are widely used tools for proactive detection of cyberattacks. However, traditional honeypots have numerous limitations in providing a real-time, deceptive environment for cyber attackers. The paper proposes a novel intelligent honeypot, LMDAHoney, that provides realistic engagements for attackers for extended periods through a series of prompt engineering techniques. It provides a shell-like framework with contextual capabilities, including adaptive deception, agents in the form of interactions, and an analytics (OIDC) module that provides a practical environment and logs all activities performed by cybercriminals. The request-response patterns in a typical shell-like environment are explored, and the LLM is trained to improve engagement. The Llama2-LLM model is leveraged and fine-tuned to simulate the request-response patterns observed in real-world interactions with attackers. The proposed model is the first of its kind to integrate modern AI technologies into a cyber deception system, thereby enhancing the security posture of digitally connected assets. The proposed method is deployed in a real-world network. The experimental results show a precision-match accuracy of 88% and a similarity score of 90%, indicating a substantial proportion of model-generated responses match realistic SQL commands and engage the attacker for longer, thereby enabling the detection of advanced cyberattacks, including malware, botnets, and advanced persistent threats (APTs).
