AB  - Agent-based automated penetration testing is an emerging paradigm in cybersecurity. Existing methods, however, often struggle with long-context understanding and exhibit limited attack diversity in complex tasks. Their generated plans also lack guarantees of logical consistency, making the testing process brittle. To address these issues, we propose PenPlan-PDDL, a novel multi-agent framework for automated penetration-test planning. The core features of this framework are its hierarchical planning architecture and a role-specialized multi-agent collaboration mechanism. More importantly, we introduce the Planning Domain Definition Language (PDDL) and its solver to perform formal verification before plan execution, thereby ensuring that every plan is logically consistent and executable. Experimental results show that PenPlan-PDDL significantly outperforms existing baselines. It improves the recall rate of MITRE ATT&CK techniques by 40%, achieves a plan-solving success rate of 97%, boosts effective tool invocations by over 30%. Our work provides an effective paradigm for building reliable automated penetration testing systems.
ER  - 

