AB  - With the evolution of Advanced Persistent Threats (APTs) and the increasingly severe cybersecurity landscape, Cyberspace Threat Intelligence (CTI) has become crucially valuable for fortifying defenses against these threats. As effective countermeasures, existing CTI-based APT provenance graph construction methods play an essential role in APT alerting and tactic detection. However, they remain primarily limited to centralized, passive detection scenarios. In this paper, we propose ActiveTrap, the first attempt at proactive APT trapping in federated scenarios by multiple AI agents. To attain proactive adversarial engagement and active deception, ActiveTrap constructs multiple AI agents powered by Large Language Models (LLMs) to autonomously generate and deploy adaptive honeypot decoys, including deceptive API endpoints and system behaviors. To achieve precise APT detection across heterogeneous, distributed edge devices, ActiveTrap introduces a CTI-based provenance hypergraph construction method coupled with a Federated Graph Learning (FGL) alert strategy. Moreover, our approach correlates and analyzes edge system logs to enable APT threat alerts and tactic detection while preserving data privacy. Empirical results demonstrate that ActiveTrap maintains high APT alert accuracy in federated scenarios, improves tactic detection accuracy by $5.9 \%$, and increases APT entrapment success rate by $39 \%$ through multiagent interaction.
