AB  - The maritime industry’s increasing digitalization has expanded the cyber attack surface of vessels and ports, yet systematic monitoring of vessel internet exposure and behavioral patterns remains limited. Port authorities lack automated tools to track vessels calling at their terminals for indicators of sanctions evasion, shadow fleet activity, or long-term behavioral anomalies, while fleet operators face challenges in assessing what information about their own vessels is publicly accessible and exploitable by adversaries. This paper presents ShipCrawler, an agentic open-source intelligence (OSINT) platform addressing both operational use cases. ShipCrawler integrates automatic identification system (AIS) feeds, internet scanning datasets (Shodan), shipping registries (Equasis), and web intelligence into an autonomous agent-driven investigation pipeline. The platform enables port authorities to conduct sustained monitoring of vessel AIS histories, ownership structures, and flag-state patterns to identify vessels operating within shadow fleet or sanctions-evasion networks, while helping fleet operators systematically enumerate their publicly visible attack surface, including exposed Very Small Aperture Terminal (VSAT) communications, bridge systems, and unsecured operational technology interfaces. A key architectural contribution is the agent-driven queue-based pipeline, where an artificial intelligence (AI) agent processes OSINT tasks asynchronously with real-time Server-Sent Events (SSE) streaming to a web dashboard. ShipCrawler has been validated on 63 crude oil tankers spanning Aframax/LR2, Suezmax, and VLCC classes within shadow fleet networks, demonstrating that sustained OSINT monitoring can successfully detect shadow fleet operational patterns, quantify vessel internet exposure, and generate structured intelligence products automatically. A discrimination test on 15 non-sanctioned control tankers drawn by a fixed-seed random sample from a non-sanctioned eligible pool exhibited zero shadow-fleet signatures, confirming the specificity of the detected indicators. The platform is released as open-source software under the MIT license.
