AB  - Artificial intelligence (AI) is undergoing a major transition from traditional discriminative models which estimate conditional probabilities to classify data and predict outcomes to Generative AI (GenAI). GenAI instead approximates joint probability distributions to synthesize novel, high-fidelity artifacts across multi-modal domains such as text, code, and imagery This work presents a comparative examination of the primary architectures underlying this transition, namely Generative Adversarial Networks (GANs), Variational Autoencoders (VAEs), Transformer architectures, and diffusion-based models. It further examines how architectural distinctions influence practical deployment across sectors. In cybersecurity, discriminative systems remain effective for anomaly detection, whereas generative models strengthen defense by simulating zero-day attack scenarios. In healthcare, generative techniques support synthetic data generation and clinical decision assistance. Despite these capabilities, generative systems introduce significant concerns, including inherited dataset bias, substantial computational demand, and hallucinated responses lacking factual grounding. The paper concludes by describing the movement toward Agentic AI autonomous entities capable of sequential reasoning and external tool utilization relying on distributed edge cloud architectures.
ER  - 

