AB  - Artificial intelligence is increasingly described as a double-edged technology in cybersecurity, yet recent work shows that its offensive potential deserves analysis in its own right. This narrative review examines how machine learning, generative models, and especially large language models are being used or evaluated as tools for cyber attacks. Drawing on recent literature discovered through Google Scholar, the paper argues that artificial intelligence changes cyber offense less by inventing wholly new attack classes than by compressing the cost of reconnaissance, phishing, adaptation, and operator support. The review concludes that the near-term risk lies in workflow acceleration, not flawless autonomy, and that defenders therefore need controls that harden both technical systems and language-heavy business processes.
ER  - 

