AB  - Web and Application Programming Interface (API) services are central to modern digital infrastructure but remain vulnerable to injection attacks, authentication bypasses, and zero-day exploits. Traditional Security Operations Center (SOC) tools rely on static, signature-based pipelines that generate high false positives, struggle with concept drift, and provide limited decision support. This paper presents ALERT (Agentic Learning for Event-driven Response and Threat Detection), a lightweight, GPU-free (CPU-only), closed-loop SOC agent with a human-in-the-loop (HITL) that integrates Common Vulnerabilities and Exposures (CVE) severity prediction, log anomaly detection, and Language Model (LLM)-assisted semantics into a cost-sensitive decision framework. ALERT ingests multimodal inputs, including vulnerability feeds, runtime logs, and analyst annotations, and dynamically adjusts responses (ALLOW, MONITOR, or BLOCK) through online policy adaptation. Evaluation on a synthetic dataset of 10,000 Apache-style logs achieved a precision of 0.991, a recall of 0.983, and an F1-score of 0.987, detecting 100 percent of critical threats while keeping false positives below 0.5 percent. Designed for scalability and transparency, ALERT’s modular architecture generalizes to Cyber-Physical Systems (CPS) and Internet of Things (IoT) telemetry, supporting practical, adaptive, and explainable SOC automation.
ER  - 

