AB  - The transition to disaggregated 5G and 6G architectures has rendered centralized security models obsolete, necessitating a shift toward decentralized, autonomous defense. While Vertical Federated Learning (VFL) offers a promising path for collaborative detection across the RAN, Edge, and Core without exposing sensitive data, it introduces a critical “trust gap.” Network operators remain hesitant to authorize autonomous mitigation actions driven by “black box” neural networks that lack transparency and accountability. This article proposes a unified framework that bridges this gap by integrating Agentic AI, VFL, and Explainable AI (XAI). We introduce a distributed architecture where agents detect attacks using privacy-preserving VFL, but crucially, leverage a Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) engine to reason before acting. By grounding AI decisions in NIST guidelines and operational Service Level Agreements (SLAs), the system transforms opaque alerts into transparent, evidence-backed mitigation plans. We demonstrate that this approach eliminates AI hallucinations and enables “surgical” responses—such as rate-limiting at the RAN or flow filtering at the Edge—that are fully traceable, policy-compliant, and trustworthy.
ER  - 

