AB  - Automobiles have developed into technologically advanced cyber-physical systems, thanks to the use of multiple Electronic Control Unit systems (ECUs) consisting of in-vehicle networking and cloud-based connectivity. These new technologies are intended to automate as well as improve safety and the user experience, but by doing so they have introduced a much larger cyber-attack surface for vehicles. Some examples of these new threats are: CAN Bus Injection, remote exploits against Infotainment Systems, GPS Spoofing, firmware alteration, abuse of over-the-air software updates, all of which directly affect your safety and privacy. Present regulations and industry standards (ISO/SAE 21434) regarding vehicle cyber-attack or generation security do not account for manual procedures associated with an increase in vulnerabilities at an exponential rate. There are no common security tools that can automatically correlate new vulnerabilities to specific vehicle systems or components and determine risk based on context. This paper describes a new framework named Automotive Shield that will provide an AI based approach to assessing vehicle cybersecurity risks in real-time by correlating new vulnerabilities to known vehicle systems or components utilizing a large language model (LLM). Automotive Shield will enable automated reporting of vulnerabilities, real-time scoring of risk associated with each vulnerability, as well as the ability to conduct vehicle cybersecurity assessments at scale for new connected & autonomous vehicles.
