AB  - Hypertext Transfer Protocol (HTTP) traffic constitutes the most prevalent traffic type on the modern internet and, consequently, serves as the primary vector for many diverse cyberattacks. Accurately identifying and categorizing these threats is essential for maintaining robust network security. In this paper we evaluate the applicability of large language models (LLM) for multi-label classification of HTTP traffic. The presented solution follows two approaches in categorizing this type of traffic and compares their efficacy when applied to public datasets and real production traffic. While in the first part of this paper we present an approach that utilizes a fine-tuned LLM with a decoder-only architecture, in the second part of this paper we focus on the automated optimization of prompts for generative LLM for achieving the same goals. These methodologies will serve as the baseline for future work, in which we intend to explore the applicability of LLM agents as a part of this classification pipeline.
ER  - 

