AB  - Cybersecurity is moving from reactive detection to proactive prediction, but current machine learning and LLM methods emphasize classification or coarse forecasting rather than predicting when a specific attack will start. We pose cyberattack prediction as a time-window localization problem, where the system must output an interval for the next attack with calibrated confidence. We introduce a hybrid retrieval-augmented, multi-agent LLM pipeline that converts network flow statistics into structured natural language context, retrieves similar historical traffic patterns from an embedding-indexed memory, and uses three specialized agents (threat analyst, quantitative estimator, and peer reviewer) to generate, refine, and validate predictions via a confidence-aware retry loop. On CIC-IDS-2017 and a zero-day logistics-network dataset, our system attains a Localization Score of 0.950 with 98% coverage over 50 trials per configuration, substantially outperforming single-agent baselines, especially under temporal ambiguity (96% vs. 36% coverage on the harder dataset). These results show that structured multi-agent reasoning with retrieval grounding markedly improves robustness, coverage, and uncertainty communication for operational attack time-window prediction.
ER  - 

