AB  - Urban digital twins (UDTs) increasingly combine building information modeling (BIM), geospatial information systems (GIS), and Internet-of-Things (IoT) sensing to support evidence-based city governance and sustainability reporting. In parallel, cities are adopting machine learning (ML) and foundation models (including large language models, LLMs) to automate indicator computation, forecasting, and decision support. Yet integrating AI into BIM-enabled UDT pipelines remains difficult due to fragmented data ecosystems, interoperability constraints, evolving regulations, and emerging security and safety risks. This paper reframes SDG-aligned UDT development as an AI integration problem. We propose a layered reference architecture for AI-enabled BIM-to-city digital twins and an end-to-end AI integration lifecycle that unifies MLOps/LLMOps practices with public-sector governance constraints. We synthesize current integration challenges in a lifecycle-oriented matrix and derive a risk–mitigation mapping grounded in recent standards and security guidance. Finally, we outline future trends agentic systems, multimodal AI, edge/on-device AI, retrieval and tool use, regulation, trustworthy AI, and evaluation/observability and discuss implications for municipal governance, sustainability decisionmaking, and building/urban-quality evaluation (e.g., daylighting, acoustics, spatial connectivity, and biophilic performance) beyond energy and carbon reporting.
ER  - 

