AB  - Phishing attacks have reached record levels, with attackers increasingly employing sophisticated evasion techniques such as cloaking, fingerprinting, and CAPTCHAs to circumvent traditional detection systems. Most existing approaches analyze webpage source code, JavaScript, and HTTP headers-artifacts that users never see and that can be manipulated to evade automated scanners. Moreover, deep learning-based detectors function as black boxes, producing accurate predictions but failing to provide interpretable explanations that build user resilience or support security analyst workflows. This disconnect between detection mechanisms and real-world incident reporting creates significant operational challenges, as users naturally capture screenshots and URLs when encountering suspicious pages through help-desk tickets or browser reporting inter-faces. We present a zero-shot explainable phishing detection system that operates exclusively on webpage screenshots and URLs-precisely the evidence users capture in practice. Our approach leverages GPT-4 Vision, a large multimodal language model, to achieve brand recognition and phishing intent detection without requiring task-specific training data. Through carefully designed chain-of-thought prompting, the system decomposes detection into interpretable reasoning steps: identifying the impersonated brand from visual and textual cues, analyzing credential request mechanisms, and detecting social engineering tactics. This structured reasoning process generates natural language explanations as integral components of detection rather than post-hoc rationalizations, making the system inherently explainable. We evaluate our system on the CIRCL Phishing Dataset. Our zero-shot approach achieves 82.0 percent accuracy on brand recognition. Additionally, our system provides an explainable AI agent that transforms technical outputs into multi-level educational explanations tailored to end users, security ana-lysts, and training programs, with visual annotations highlighting suspicious elements. Our work demonstrates that zero-shot large language models can achieve state-of-the-art phishing detection while providing inherent explainability, immediate deployment capability, and adaptability to emerging threats through prompt engineering. By aligning with real-world incident response work-flows and transforming detection from reactive blacklisting into proactive user education, this approach addresses critical gaps in both accuracy and interpretability for practical cybersecurity defense.
ER  - 

