AB  - This study presents a security-oriented evaluation of the Model Context Protocol (MCP), an open standard enabling large language models (LLMs) to interface with external tools through structured interactions. The experiment was deployed in a controlled environment using an open-source MCP server with basic file manipulation tools. A set of prompt injection tests was designed to probe the system's ability to handle adversarial instructions, ambiguous input, and encoded payloads. Despite expectations, the system failed to reject adversarial instructions embedded in user input and did not detect anomalous tool usage patterns-such as suspicious parameters. These failures underscore the current vulnerability of LLM-tool integrations to prompt injection threats. The results highlight the urgent need for defense-in-depth strategies within MCP implementations, including stricter input validation, contextaware filtering, strong access control policies and transparent logging. Collectively, these measures are essential for securing AI-assisted tool chains against emerging adversarial techniques.
ER  - 

