AB  - As virtual mental health adoption continues to grow, more sensitive patient information is becoming an easy target of cyber threats, such as unauthorized access, information leaks, and privacy breaches. The security and integrity of these online therapy sessions would be vital and especially so given that patients with chronic conditions such as diabetes, thyroid, and mental issues are conducting online sessions. The paper introduces a zero-trust AI-based architecture of cybersecurity system in virtual therapy to implement privacy and policy adherence and identify and prevent real-time breaches of privacy and unusual user activity. To do that, text-based communications are analyzed with the help of large language models (LLaMA 3.2 3B and Med42 v2 8B) to determine the possible data leak or breach of privacy. An encryption algorithm based on the block-wise concept with a lightweight is used in securing sensitive communications and deep reinforcement learning (DRL) is adopted in identifying anomalies in the user activity and access patterns to the system. A simulated environment of 25 synthetic patient sessions used to sample anonymized session logs, access records, communication logs and threat detection metrics and then preprocessed such that identifiable information was removed and forms uniformed before being used to train and test the models. The architecture had 94.7% accuracy in anomaly detection and 87% less exposure of data but preserved integrity of the session, imposed privacy laws, and allowed automated response, which proves the efficiency of AI and cryptographic protection against virtual mental health platforms.
ER  - 

