AB  - OT environments keep industrial assets in service far longer than typical IT systems, which increases cybersecurity exposure and forces remediation decisions to rely on evidence scattered across vendor bulletins, advisories, and release notes. Assembling this evidence into a coherent profile, a task referred to as CVE profiling, requires matching affected versions, identifying exploit preconditions, and reconciling remediation guidance across sources that differ in scope and authority. Applying off-the-shelf LLM-based deep-research systems is a natural starting point, but their outputs are often unreliable. In this work, we identify five core reliability challenges and design guardrails to address them in two LLM-based variants: a prompt-controlled agent (soft approach), which encodes the discipline through instructions, and a workflow with runtime controls (hard approach), which enforces it through explicit gates. Evaluated on a 24-case industrial benchmark spanning controllers, supervisory software, networking devices, and cross-cutting component vulnerabilities, both variants outperform a generic baseline across all metrics. Beyond the overall gains, each variant shows strengths in distinct quality dimensions. The soft variant leads on profile completeness and factual quality, improving Coverage and Factualness by 25% and 20% over the baseline, while hard variant leads on evidence grounding, raising Grounded Correctness by 36%. Together, these results show that structured guardrails can make LLM-based deep research substantially more reliable for vulnerability management, and that soft variant can serve as a competitive, lightweight alternative to workflow enforcement when grounding requirements are moderate.
