AB  - Security monitoring has become more important in recent years because weapons and explosive devices can be misused in public spaces. Places like airports, train stations, and large events need constant observation to prevent potential dangers. In many cases, security staff watch surveillance cameras, but continuous manual monitoring can be difficult and might lead to missed incidents. Therefore, researchers are looking into automated systems to assist human operators. This work presents a detection system that identifies bombs and weapons in surveillance images and drone footage. The MobileNetV2 deep learning architecture, employing transfer learning, serves as the foundation for the model. Prior to the training phase, the acquired images were subjected to resizing, normalization, and data augmentation techniques to enhance the model's learning capacity. Subsequent to training, the system's efficacy is assessed through a range of performance metrics, including accuracy, precision, recall, and ROC analysis. The findings indicate the model's capacity to accurately identify security threats while differentiating them from benign objects. Consequently, the system offers valuable support to security personnel by automating image analysis and flagging potentially suspicious items. This approach has the potential to accelerate monitoring processes and improve safety protocols in surveillance environments.
