AB  - Cyber Threat Intelligence (CTI) plays a pivotal role in cybersecurity by enabling proactive threat knowledge sharing. Within this ecosystem, Tactics, Techniques, and Procedures (TTPs) serve as fundamental behavioral patterns that allow defenders to anticipate adversarial actions. However, effectively extracting TTPs remains a persistent challenge because they are embedded in complex, unstructured natural language reports. Current approaches face significant limitations: discriminative models generalize poorly to unseen textual descriptions of known attack behaviors, while generative approaches based on Large Language Models (LLMs) suffer from hallucinations and lack interpretability. To address these issues, we present TTP-MAS, a hierarchical multi-agent framework grounded in Dempster-Shafer (D-S) Evidence Theory. Unlike conventional pipelines, TTP-MAS orchestrates a judicial arbitration framework. Within this system, a specialized Correction Expert acts as an adversarial auditor by leveraging ‘negative evidence’ to challenge potential errors, while a Chief Justice agent resolves conflicts to enforce global logical consistency. Experiments on AnnoCTR and TRAM2 datasets show that TTP-MAS achieves superior TTP extraction performance, outperforming the strong baseline TTPXHunter by an F1-Score margin of 6.65% ∼ 10.13%. Furthermore, TTP-MAS provides granular provenance mappings for verifiable and interpretable extraction.
