AB  - Cybersecurity is becoming paramount in energy grids and markets. In line with these trends, this paper introduces a collaborative mitigation model for energy grids that builds on the group decision-making process for cyberattack mitigation. It first defines a multi-criteria decision model for the energy sector that considers energy-related criteria for the efficiency of mitigation strategies and the impacts of cyber threats. It then advances the process with a multi-agent protocol based on an integrative multi-lateral negotiation algorithm to automate group decision-making. Agents define mitigation strategies to address cyberattack vectors and vulnerabilities affecting assets within the energy grid. Based on consensus and penalty measures, agents seek an optimal cost-effective strategy that minimizes impact on all stakeholders, i.e., asset owners, grid operators, consumers, and producers. The core mitigation system is integrated with several external components for incident detection and vulnerability identification, including anomaly detection systems, common repositories, collaborative platforms, and large language models. The model is evaluated based on two use cases for the energy grid. First, we integrate it with the cooperative negotiation model for self-sustainable energy communities. Second, we apply it on diverse organizational levels to select mitigation countermeasures to strengthen the resilience of a SCADA-supervised energy network.
ER  - 

