AB  - Large Language Models (LLMs) have transformed the ability of attackers to compromise authentication processes through the autonomous generation of adaptive attacks. Objectives: This research aims to develop computational cognitive profiles that simulate human behavior to bypass automated detection, and to empirically analyze password patterns based on the Ecuadorian context. Method: We employed an iterative Secure Software Development Life Cycle (SSDLC) methodology across four iterations to develop and validate these offensive and defensive contributions. Results: We developed three differentiated cognitive profiles (reflective novice, continuous expert, and erratic automated) validated against temporal distributions of human interaction, achieving a 91% evasion rate of detection systems. The analysis of 207 passwords reveals exploitable cultural patterns: 66% incorporate numerical suffixes, 20% include local contextual references, and the average length is 10.5 characters. This work establishes the foundations for resilient authentication systems that consider cognitive and cultural factors.
