AB  - The increasing complexity of cyber threats requires security systems that can interpret attacker behavior and respond autonomously. This paper presents a cognitive honeypot framework that integrates a large language model into a medium-interaction honeypot to enable real-time semantic threat classification and mitigation. The system captures SSH and HTTP session data, converts interaction logs into structured prompts, and uses a pre-trained model to analyze intent using natural language understanding. Based on the model's output, the framework assigns risk scores and automatically enforces firewall rules to block malicious IP addresses without relying on manual supervision or static rules. In a two-week deployment on a publicly accessible cloud instance, the system recorded 357 sessions, identified 126 as malicious, and achieved 97.75% mitigation accuracy with a median response time of 0.95 seconds. These results demonstrate the feasibility of integrating language models into honeypot systems to enable adaptive, autonomous defense capabilities in live environments. Evaluation was limited to 357 live SSH and HTTP sessions over two weeks; ongoing work aims to extend validation to encrypted traffic (TLS/HTTPS), multi-node deployments, and larger cross-domain datasets to assess statistical generalization
ER  - 

