AB  - As a key technology that enabled the evolution of traditional grids to next generation smart grids, internet of things has also enabled smart grids to have an expanded attack surface. This paper addresses the problem that Internet of Things are not given immediate network cybersecurity defenses once joining a smart grid which is a major vulnerability. The proposed hybrid machine learning and deep learning-based autonomous decision-making system framework will focus on implementing instant network segmentation configurations deduced from IoT sensor data and network telemetry data. This system is evaluated on a framework that utilizes a testbed that involves network telemetry data relating to cyber-attacks while utilizing a GPT-2–based small language model implemented using the HuggingFace Transformers framework. The hybrid model performed well overall for the logistic regression model and small language model. Results show a near-perfect ROC-AUC performance across evaluated attack classes from the logistic regression models, while the small language model has a perplexity of 1.02 and a TPOT of 31.6 nanoseconds indicating it is very confident and timely in the next token selection. This study supports the concept that instant network segmentation can be achieved for Internet of Thingsenabled smart grids with autonomous network-based defense mechanisms. In this context, ‘instant’ refers to near-real-time policy selection following packet-level inference without human intervention.
ER  - 

