AB  - Artificial Intelligence (AI) is transforming cybersecurity by enhancing both defensive capabilities and offensive cyber operations. While most research focuses on AI-enabled attacks targeting higher layers of the communication stack, less attention has been given to the Network Access Layer, which supports Ethernet, Wi-Fi, MAC addressing, switching infrastructures, and address-resolution protocols. This paper examines how AI amplifies traditional Network Access Layer threats through intelligent reconnaissance, behavioral profiling, device impersonation, protocol exploitation, wireless attacks, radio-frequency intelligence gathering, and large-scale IoT targeting. A taxonomy of AI-driven threats is presented and linked to enabling machine-learning techniques, including reinforcement learning, deep learning, adversarial machine learning, generative AI, federated learning exploitation, and graph analytics. The paper analyzes vulnerabilities affecting hardware, wireless communications, communication protocols, trust models, IoT environments, and AI-based security systems. To address these challenges, a multilayer defense framework is proposed that combines AI-driven intrusion detection, behavioral analytics, explainable AI, Zero Trust principles, secure access-layer controls, and automated response capabilities. The findings demonstrate that AI is fundamentally reshaping the threat landscape at the Network Access Layer and requires adaptive, intelligence-driven security architectures.
ER  - 

